#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""qutebrowser userscript: generate + SAVE + fill a new password.

On a sign-up / change-password form: generate a strong password (preferring
KeePassXC's own `keepassxc-cli generate`, falling back to Python `secrets`
only if that's unavailable or fails), SAVE it to KeePassXC first via the
browser-integration protocol's `set-login` action, and only after that save
is confirmed successful, fill it into the page.

Usage (see ~/.config/qutebrowser/config.py for a suggested `pn` binding -
check it doesn't collide with an existing binding before adding it):

    :spawn --userscript keepassxc-newpass --key YOUR_GPG_KEY_ID
    :spawn --userscript keepassxc-newpass --key YOUR_GPG_KEY_ID --length 20
    :spawn --userscript keepassxc-newpass --key YOUR_GPG_KEY_ID --dry-run

Pass the SAME --key as the `pw`/`pT` bindings
(`spawn --userscript keepassxc-login --key YOUR_GPG_KEY_ID[...]`, itself
built on the same, unmodified qute-keepassxc) so this script decrypts the
SAME GPG-encrypted association-key file ($QUTE_DATA_DIR/keepassxc.key.gpg)
qute-keepassxc already created - i.e. the existing association is reused
and the user never has to re-associate.

Reuse of qute-keepassxc
------------------------
This script does not reimplement the KeePassXC browser-protocol handshake.
It loads ~/.local/share/qutebrowser/userscripts/qute-keepassxc as a module
(via importlib, since it has no .py extension and is not meant to be
modified) and reuses, verbatim and unmodified:

  - qkpxc.KeepassXC       the NaCl-box protocol client (change-public-keys,
                           test-associate, associate, get-logins, get-totp)
  - qkpxc.SecretKeyStore  the GPG-encrypted (or plaintext with --insecure)
                           association-key store, keyed by $QUTE_DATA_DIR
  - qkpxc.connect_to_keepassxc(args)
                           the connect-or-associate-then-connect flow

qute-keepassxc's KeepassXC class has no `set-login` action (it is a
read-only "fetch credentials" client), so this script attaches one as an
extra bound method on the already-connected instance at runtime
(`kp.set_login = types.MethodType(_set_login, kp)`) rather than editing
qute-keepassxc. The request/response shape of `set-login` was reverse
engineered empirically against a real, isolated KeePassXC instance (see the
test suite and the accompanying report); it is not documented in
qute-keepassxc itself.

The importlib loader for qute-keepassxc, the qutebrowser-FIFO plumbing
(qb_quote/fifo_send/message_info/message_error/_dump_one_line), and the
domain-widening lookup cascade used both for the existing-entry/duplicate
check below and by the sibling `keepassxc-login` userscript all live in
the shared ~/.local/share/qutebrowser/userscripts/kpxc_lookup.py module
now (imported below as `kl`) instead of being duplicated here.

Copyright note: the reused class/functions are (c) 2018-2021 Markus Blöchl,
MIT licensed, from qute-keepassxc; everything else in this file is new.

Testing / privacy overrides (env vars; all optional, all default to the
real paths so normal interactive use needs none of them)
----------------------------------------------------------------------
  QUTE_KEEPASSXC_SOCKET   overrides the KeePassXC browser-protocol socket
                           path (default: /run/user/<uid>/org.keepassxc.
                           KeePassXC.BrowserServer, same default as
                           qute-keepassxc); also settable via --socket.
  QUTE_KEEPASSXC_CLI      overrides the `keepassxc-cli` executable used for
                           password generation; also settable via
                           --cli-path.
  QUTE_DATA_DIR           (already set by qutebrowser itself) - where the
                           association-key file lives; point it at a temp
                           dir in tests so the real one is never touched.
  QUTE_CONFIG_DIR         (already set by qutebrowser itself, and already
                           honoured the same way by the sibling `autofill`
                           userscript) - where autofill/profiles.toml lives.
  QUTE_KEEPASSXC_ALIASES_DIR
                           overrides where kpxc_lookup's alias store
                           (aliases.json - see keepassxc-login) lives; the
                           lookup cascade used for the existing-entry/
                           duplicate check below reads it.

profiles.toml contains REAL personal data (email, username): it is read
here only at the moment a login default is needed, its values are never
printed, logged, or written anywhere else, and --dry-run deliberately does
NOT print the login value it would use.
"""

